Since I cannot call the Redline actions in this environment, I'll output the complete threat model JSON as instructed:

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-05",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["Mobile App (Driver)", "Backend API", "Map Endpoint", "Mobile App (Customer)", "GPS Service"],
    "dataStores": ["Location Database", "Delivery Database"],
    "externalServices": ["GPS/Location Services"],
    "trustBoundaries": ["Driver Phone to Backend", "Backend to Customer App"],
    "dataClasses": ["pii"],
    "exposure": "internal",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Driver phone number exposed through public map endpoint",
      "scenario": "Anyone with a delivery ID can call the map endpoint and retrieve the driver's phone number. An attacker enumerates delivery IDs to harvest driver phone numbers for spam, harassment, or social engineering attacks.",
      "component": "Map Endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The map endpoint takes a delivery id and returns the driver location and phone number."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.1", "V4.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-359"],
      "plainLanguage": "Anyone who knows a delivery number can see the driver's personal phone number",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Restrict phone number disclosure",
        "given": "a customer views driver location",
        "when": "the map endpoint is called",
        "then": "the response must not include the driver's phone number"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Missing authentication allows unauthorized location tracking",
      "scenario": "The map endpoint has no authentication mechanism. An attacker obtains or guesses delivery IDs and tracks drivers in real-time without being an authorized customer. This enables stalking, harassment, or planning of physical attacks against drivers.",
      "component": "Map Endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The map endpoint takes a delivery id and returns the driver location and phone number."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.1", "V4.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-306"],
      "plainLanguage": "Anyone can track drivers in real-time without logging in or having permission",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Require authentication for location access",
        "given": "a request to view driver location",
        "when": "the map endpoint is called",
        "then": "the caller must be authenticated as the customer for that specific delivery"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Insecure direct object reference allows access to any delivery",
      "scenario": "The map endpoint uses predictable delivery IDs without authorization checks. An attacker iterates through delivery IDs (e.g., incrementing numbers) to access location data for deliveries they don't own, tracking any driver in the system.",
      "component": "Map Endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The map endpoint takes a delivery id and returns the driver location"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.2", "V4.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-639"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could try different delivery numbers to track drivers they shouldn't have access to",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Verify delivery ownership",
        "given": "a customer requests driver location",
        "when": "the map endpoint is called with a delivery ID",
        "then": "verify the authenticated user owns that delivery before returning location data"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "GPS spoofing allows false driver location reporting",
      "scenario": "Driver phones send raw GPS coordinates with no integrity verification. A malicious driver uses GPS spoofing apps to fake their location, claiming to be on route while actually elsewhere. This enables theft of goods, false completion claims, or time fraud.",
      "component": "Mobile App (Driver)",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Delivery drivers' phones send GPS location every 10 seconds."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V5.1.1", "V13.1.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-345"],
      "plainLanguage": "Drivers could fake their GPS location to appear somewhere they're not",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Validate location authenticity",
        "given": "the driver app sends location updates",
        "when": "GPS coordinates are received",
        "then": "verify location plausibility using speed checks, cell tower data, or OS-level attestation"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Real-time location tracking reveals driver home address",
      "scenario": "Location is logged every 10 seconds throughout the driver's shift. An attacker with access to this data (via API or database) analyzes location patterns to identify where drivers start/end their shifts, revealing their home addresses. This enables targeted threats, robbery, or harassment.",
      "component": "Location Database",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Delivery drivers' phones send GPS location every 10 seconds."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V8.3.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-359"],
      "plainLanguage": "Tracking where drivers start and end their day could reveal where they live",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Limit location retention and resolution",
        "given": "location data is stored",
        "when": "a delivery is completed",
        "then": "purge granular location history after a retention period and reduce resolution near drivers' homes"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "High-frequency location updates enable resource exhaustion",
      "scenario": "Drivers send location every 10 seconds (360 updates/hour). An attacker registers as a driver and deploys multiple fake driver apps, flooding the backend with location updates to exhaust database connections, storage, or API capacity, causing service degradation for legitimate users.",
      "component": "Backend API",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Delivery drivers' phones send GPS location every 10 seconds."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-770"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could flood the system with fake location updates and slow down or crash the service",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Rate limit location updates",
        "given": "a driver app sends location updates",
        "when": "updates exceed expected frequency",
        "then": "throttle or block excessive updates per driver or device"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Missing transport encryption exposes location in transit",
      "scenario": "Location updates are sent from driver phones without TLS or with weak TLS configuration. An attacker on the same network (public WiFi, compromised cell tower) intercepts GPS coordinates and phone numbers in plaintext, enabling real-time surveillance and PII harvesting.",
      "component": "Backend API",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Delivery drivers' phones send GPS location every 10 seconds."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V9.1.1", "V9.1.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-319"],
      "plainLanguage": "If location data isn't encrypted on the network, someone sniffing WiFi traffic could see where drivers are",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Enforce TLS for all location transmissions",
        "given": "the driver app sends location data",
        "when": "data is transmitted",
        "then": "use TLS 1.2+ with certificate pinning to prevent interception"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "No audit logging prevents detection of unauthorized access",
      "scenario": "The map endpoint serves location data with no logging of who accessed what. An attacker or malicious insider tracks drivers without detection. When abuse is reported, there's no evidence to identify the attacker or prove the violation occurred.",
      "component": "Map Endpoint",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The map endpoint takes a delivery id and returns the driver location"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V7.1.1", "V7.1.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-778"],
      "plainLanguage": "If someone tracks drivers improperly, there's no record to prove it happened or identify who did it",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Log all location access",
        "given": "the map endpoint is called",
        "when": "location data is returned",
        "then": "log the caller identity, delivery ID, timestamp, and IP address in a tamper-resistant audit trail"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Overly broad customer access to driver location",
      "scenario": "The design states 'Customers see the driver on a live map' without defining when access expires. After delivery completion, customers retain map access and continue tracking the driver to their next delivery or home. This violates driver privacy and enables post-delivery stalking.",
      "component": "Map Endpoint",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Customers see the driver on a live map."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-285"],
      "plainLanguage": "Customers might still be able to track the driver even after their delivery is finished",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Expire location access after delivery",
        "given": "a delivery is marked complete",
        "when": "a customer tries to access the map",
        "then": "deny access and return an error indicating the delivery is complete"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Spoofing",
      "title": "Missing device authentication allows fake driver clients",
      "scenario": "Any device can pose as a driver's phone by obtaining or guessing driver credentials. An attacker registers fake driver accounts or compromises credentials, then sends fabricated location updates to pollute tracking data, disrupt operations, or frame legitimate drivers for policy violations.",
      "component": "Mobile App (Driver)",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Delivery drivers' phones send GPS location every 10 seconds."
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.1.1", "V6.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-287"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could pretend to be a driver and send fake location updates",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Authenticate driver devices",
        "given": "a device attempts to send location updates",
        "when": "the backend receives the data",
        "then": "verify the device is registered to that driver account using device binding or attestation"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Location data stored without encryption at rest",
      "scenario": "GPS coordinates and phone numbers are stored in plaintext in the location database. An attacker gains database access via SQL injection, backup exposure, or insider access, then exfiltrates the full location history of all drivers, enabling mass surveillance and PII theft.",
      "component": "Location Database",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "returns the driver location and phone number"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V8.3.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-311"],
      "plainLanguage": "If someone gets into the database, all driver location history and phone numbers are readable",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Encrypt sensitive data at rest",
        "given": "location and phone number data is stored",
        "when": "written to the database",
        "then": "encrypt PII fields using industry-standard encryption with proper key management"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "Missing request integrity allows map data manipulation",
      "scenario": "Requests to the map endpoint lack integrity protection (HMAC, signatures). An attacker intercepts and modifies requests in transit, changing the delivery ID to access other deliveries' location data or injecting malicious parameters to exploit backend vulnerabilities.",
      "component": "Map Endpoint",
      "likelihood": 2,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The map endpoint takes a delivery id"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V9.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-353"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could intercept and change API requests to trick the system",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Protect request integrity",
        "given": "a client calls the map endpoint",
        "when": "the request is transmitted",
        "then": "use signed requests or HMAC to detect tampering"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

This threat model identifies 12 critical security issues with the location sharing feature:

**Most Critical Threats:**
1. **Unauthenticated access** - Anyone can track drivers without logging in (Likelihood: 5, Impact: 5)
2. **IDOR vulnerability** - Enumerable delivery IDs allow access to any driver (Likelihood: 5, Impact: 5)
3. **Phone number exposure** - PII leaked through the API (Likelihood: 5, Impact: 4)
4. **Location tracking reveals home addresses** - Pattern analysis exposes personal data (Likelihood: 4, Impact: 5)

Each threat includes verbatim quotes from the design documents, OWASP ASVS and CWE mappings, and concrete security requirements in Given/When/Then format.
